<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thessalonike of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 352–295 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thessalonike of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and half-sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> at Pydna in 316 BCE, she was married to him, strengthening his claim to the Macedonian throne. Cassander named the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> after her, and she was killed by her own son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> in 295 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thessalonike-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Thessalonike of Macedon" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philly boy92 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thessaloniki-ancient_inscription.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
